{"name":"Peacock Tail","hex":"#01636d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"peacock","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A male peafowl, especially Pavo cristatus, notable for its brilliant iridescently ocellated tail.","A peafowl (of the genus Pavo or Afropavo), either male or female."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To strut about proudly or haughtily.","To engage in peacocking, ostentatious dress or behaviour to impress women."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peacock"},{"term":"tail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The caudal appendage of an animal that is attached to their posterior and near the anus or cloaca.","An object or part of an object resembling a tail in shape, such as the thongs on a cat-o'-nine-tails; a strand of material hanging from something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hold by the end; said of a timber when it rests upon a wall or other support; with in or into","To swing with the stern in a certain direction; said of a vessel at anchor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Peafowl","blocks":[{"text":"Peafowl is a common name for two bird species of the genus Pavo and one species of the closely related genus Afropavo within the tribe Pavonini of the family Phasianidae. Male peafowl are referred to as peacocks, and female peafowl are referred to as peahens. Despite this, peacock is usually used to refer to both sexes, in common language."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Peacock_Plumage.jpg/330px-Peacock_Plumage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peafowl","wikidata":"Q201251"},{"title":"Tail","blocks":[{"text":"The tail is the elongated section at the rear end of a bilaterian animal's body; in general, the term refers to a distinct, flexible appendage extending backwards from the midline of the torso. In vertebrate animals that evolved to lose their tails, the coccyx is the homologous vestigial of the tail. While tails are primarily considered a feature of vertebrates, some invertebrates such as scorpions and springtails, as well as snails and slugs, have tail-like appendages that are also referred to as tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg/330px-White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail","wikidata":"Q60960"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Eggs_of_Peafowl_at_Aravath_Kasaragod.jpg/500px-Eggs_of_Peafowl_at_Aravath_Kasaragod.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Eggs_of_Peafowl_at_Aravath_Kasaragod.jpg/500px-Eggs_of_Peafowl_at_Aravath_Kasaragod.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEggs_of_Peafowl_at_Aravath_Kasaragod.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg/500px-Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg/1280px-Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg/500px-Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABaby_Peacock_(18131813108).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Peacock-JS.jpg/500px-Peacock-JS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parse